A||||15||Big O||A Diamond In the Rough||||10||
Z||000000||Big O||04-15-2000||10:31 PM||||Had an interesting conversation in the tire store today.  I recently had an article in the local Modesto Bee featuring my 86 GT.  A customer stopped in today to get a flat tire repaired (he wound up getting four new tires)and in the process he mentioned he had seen the article in the paper.  He said he was thinking about contacting me because he also is interested in Fieros.  It seems he has one for sale.  It is an 85 Indy pace car that he had customized several years ago to be a show car.  It has been in storage for several years and he tells me he has over $50,000.00 invested in it.  He was just at the point of finishing the job when he went through a messy divorce and put the car in storage.  I haven't seen it yet, but he tells me that the engine was turned over to Pontiac Motorsport for rebuild and that he has over $8,000.00 invested in it alone.  There was also a great deal of cash spent on gold and crome plating and a custom interior.  It is a 2.8 with the 4 speed.  The engine has been ported and polished and he tells me that it has been dynoed at 200 hp. He says he wants to get rid of it CHEAP so he can pick up a vintage Corvette.  He says it needs to be paintd and that due to the long time in storage some of the chrome plating is starting to deteriorate. I've asked him to get me a detailed description and maybe some pictures.  If I don't pick this one up myself I will post the info on the forum so someone else will have a chance to get it.  The car is located in Turlock and I think the guy is legit.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Phil||04-15-2000||10:57 PM||||I sure I will be corrected if wrong but I believe the Indy Pace car was only in 84 and it was a 4cyl. Also 200hp out of a 2.8 without either nitrous or forced induction is a pretty lofty number. I also would be leary of the gold/chrome plating for there is not any metal trim on the exterior and darn little on the interior. Sounds like  the old Lotus suspension and factory turbo lets impress the kid story.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 04-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||04-15-2000||11:05 PM||||I'm pretty sure Phil's right about the Indy only being available in 84.. and it was a 4cyl. (actual pace car was a SD4 I think.)<P>However I also used to think the SEs were only available with a V6 (until I remembered the 84 SE.. no V6s until 85) but I was wrong about that, so I guess anything's possible.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 04-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||04-15-2000||11:14 PM||||Hmmm, my 85 SE is a 4 cyl. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||04-15-2000||11:41 PM||||All 84 Indy pace cars were 4cyl.<BR>85 SE was 4cyl as standard but you could get the V-6 as an option.<BR> The 85 pace car was a "PPG" pace car. There was no pace car's sold to the public. (like the 84 Indy's) According to Automobile Magazine (Oct 85) GM made 10 pace cars in 1985 using the 2.8L V-6 with a modified intake system and heads. If the car he has is a REAL PPG pace car it is either blueish purple or red and the lower piece of the aero nose is missing so it looks like one big cut-out. These pace cars were used in PPG sponsored races. Ususally the Fiero PPG cars were used for the GTU classes. This was later replaced by the Chrysler PPG car based on the Daytona body. The Chrysler PPG was the car glorified in the movie "The Wraith". I wish there was a way to track down all the REAL pace cars that were used. I know there were 5 original SD4 Indy Pace cars. 1 is at Indy in the museum and the other is in the Pontiac Museum. But where did the other 3 go? PPG had 10 cars and it is rumored that there were 2 other Fiero pace cars made with the GT fastback body but I don't know what they used for an engine. These were used for races that had the "ENTAC Fiero race cars"<BR>I think that was 1987ish.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||04-15-2000||11:57 PM||||The actual 1984 Indy Pace car had the super duty  engine which I understood put out 200+hp. I think it was a 4 cylinder one at that.  Not sold to the public.  Joe Wynman in conversation had said to redo that engine would cost $8000-$10000 if you could get the parts.<P>Any need for replating is expensive.<P>Fran Passsute has a highly modified turbo charged 1984 Pace car (converted to V-6 )with aero packages and fast pack.  More modifications that you can imagine.  He usually shows up at FOCOA nationals plus some other shows.  A real nice car that Fran put big $s into.  <P>The guy could have put that much into his car, but who is willing to buy it for that much?||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||04-16-2000||01:19 AM||||Uhh,dad, define "let go of it cheap". <BR>If he's got 50 large into it, how <BR>cheap could it be?  I would be <BR>interested in seeing what kind of <BR>modifications it has.  I'm thinking <BR>gold wire wheels, crushed veluor <BR>interior and hydraulics on all four <BR>wheels. Side bet on the airbrushed <BR>bikini girl on the hood!  :P<P>P.S. Congrats on making the news. Mail me a copy of the article!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Big O||04-16-2000||01:52 AM||||Well I guess I had it coming to me, passing on barbershop gossip and all.  It appears that I'm as ignorant of "Indy" Pace Cars as the guy I was talking to.  Well, trust the group to educate me.  As to what the car really looks like, I haven't seen it so I don't know.  Next time I'll keep my mouth shut until I have all the facts correct.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||04-16-2000||09:08 AM||||Hey Big-O, that's what the Forum is for, to learn. Maybe you misunderstood the year, or maybe that guy was B.S.ing you. Now you are better informed if you are seriously going to consider buying it. I don't know what you could do to a 2.8 that would cost 8 grand. I think that guy either is pulling your leg or has way more money than brains.||||reg||1
